// @run
// @log o
// @log s
// @log 5
// @log x
// Regression (silent-class-inventory D943).
//
// A generic struct holding BOTH a `T` field and a closure capturing the `T` value. The
// SECOND ref instantiation emitted invalid wasm — `type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref
// $type)` — and the disassembly named it exactly: both `mk` instances built their closure
// environment with `struct.new $10`, where `$10` is `(struct (field i32))`, minted from the
// FIRST instantiation. The `T := string` instance then stored a string ref into an i32 field.
//
// THREE THINGS WERE MISSING, and only the first is where the row pointed.
//
// 1. THE CLONE GATE READ THE WRITTEN SIGNATURE, NOT THE CAPTURES. `monoLamNodeIsTemplate`
//    asks whether a parameter or the return still names a type parameter. `show: () =>
//    string` names none — it only CAPTURES a `T`. One lifted slot means one env struct
//    (`fnEnvIdx` is parallel to `fnStmts`), so the shared lambda could only ever have one
//    layout. A capture is part of a lambda's effective signature.
//
// 2. THE WALKER NEVER REACHED THIS LAMBDA. `monoPinBodyLambdas` documented its own bound —
//    "only a `LetDecl` INITIALIZER is rewritten" — and this lambda is a FIELD VALUE in an
//    object literal returned from the generic. That is the shape a generic BUILDER has, so
//    the bound excluded the main case. An `ObjLit` arm was the fix, recursing as well as
//    rewriting.
//
// 3. THE CLONE INHERITED THE TEMPLATE'S FRAME. `fnParent` cannot be set at clone time: the
//    instance's own `FuncDecl` does not exist yet, because its body is what the walk is
//    producing. So the clone pointed at the template, `captureValKind` resolved `value` to
//    `value: T` there, and both instances minted the same env layout — the clone existed and
//    changed nothing. The minted slots are now recorded and re-pointed at the instance the
//    moment it is lifted.
//
// Any one of the three alone leaves the witness failing; the second and third each produce a
// build that looks like progress and prints the same error.
type Box<T> = { actual: T, show: () => string }

function mk<T>(value: T): Box<T> {
  return {
    actual: value,
    show: () => {
      if value is string { return "s" }
      "o"
    },
  }
}

const b = mk(5)
const f = b.show
print(f())

const b2 = mk("x")
const f2 = b2.show
print(f2())

// The `T` FIELD still reads back at both instantiations — the env fix must not cost the
// struct field its own per-instance layout, which is the other half of the same collision.
print(b.actual)
print(b2.actual)
